{"custom_id": "87c09941b5856958bbf9b84023db3b442e7a9eafd6d6b6f7", "meta": {"loop": "S+-r01-S+-10192393", "task": "annotate_with_guidelines", "occurrence": 0, "attempt": 0, "max_tokens": 128000, "prompt_sha256": "91fbac8a59ed89b2d771f8c01e46eda880a14d6778499ed21ad48de6325d42d9", "prompt_chars": 10525}, "batch_id": "msgbatch_01CExDSRTBhn68TSGCQpcsij", "saved_utc": "2026-11-13T22:23:01+00:00", "message": {"id": "msg_011Ch1eax2iqedJghCkddpRa", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "Now I'm working through which disease terms in the sentence to tag, weighing whether \"skin tumour\" is a class or a specific disease and how to treat the gene and protein names that appear nearby.\n\n", "type": "thinking"}, {"text": "{\"annotations\":[{\"start\":15,\"end\":26,\"text\":\"skin tumour\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"'Skin tumour' refers to a family of skin tumours rather than one specific disease. The term 'tumour' is retained under the guidelines.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What NOT to Annotate §4 (tumor retained)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":455,\"end\":461,\"text\":\"cancer\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"'Cancer' is a broad family of diseases. Although general, 'cancer' is explicitly retained by the guidelines.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What NOT to Annotate §4 (cancer retained)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":496,\"end\":509,\"text\":\"colon cancers\",\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'Colon cancer' names one specific disease (colorectal cancer of the colon) with a single definition.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":552,\"end\":578,\"text\":\"adenomatous polyposis coli\",\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"Here the phrase is the name of the APC gene product (protein), not a disease. It is included because it is a disease name under some reading, but it is excluded in this context as a non-disease biological entity.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate §5 (non-disease biological entity, applied by analogy)\",\"excluded\":\"yes\"},{\"start\":687,\"end\":715,\"text\":\"colon and some other cancers\",\"entity\":\"CompositeMention\",\"rationale\":\"A contiguous string containing two separate disease mentions: colon cancer and other cancers. Annotated as one Composite Mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §2\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":875,\"end\":887,\"text\":\"skin tumours\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"'Skin tumours' refers to a class of tumours resembling pilomatricomas, not one specific disease. 'Tumour' is retained.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What NOT to Annotate §4 (tumor retained)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":899,\"end\":913,\"text\":\"pilomatricomas\",\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"'Pilomatricomas' is a single specific benign skin tumour (pilomatricoma) with a defined definition.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":1043,\"end\":1057,\"text\":\"pilomatricomas\",\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"Second occurrence of the specific disease pilomatricoma. The guidelines require annotating duplicate mentions.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What to Annotate §4 (duplicates)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":1234,\"end\":1245,\"text\":\"skin tumour\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"Repeated mention of the skin tumour family. Duplicate mentions are annotated, and 'tumour' is retained.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What to Annotate §4 (duplicates); What NOT to Annotate §4 (tumor retained)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":1287,\"end\":1293,\"text\":\"tumour\",\"entity\":\"Modifier\",\"rationale\":\"'Tumour' is used as a modifier of 'cells' (tumour cells). It is a disease term modifying another noun phrase.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §3\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":1337,\"end\":1351,\"text\":\"pilomatricomas\",\"entity\":\"SpecificDisease\",\"rationale\":\"Third occurrence of the specific disease pilomatricoma. Duplicate mentions are annotated.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What to Annotate §4 (duplicates)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":1412,\"end\":1419,\"text\":\"tumours\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"'These tumours' refers to pilomatricomas as a group of tumours. 'Tumour' is retained, and the term is a class-level mention.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What NOT to Annotate §4 (tumor retained)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":1642,\"end\":1649,\"text\":\"tumours\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"'Other human tumours' is a general class of tumours. 'Human' is an organism term and is excluded, but 'tumours' is retained.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What to Annotate §1; What NOT to Annotate §4 (tumor retained)\",\"excluded\":\"no\"},{\"start\":1765,\"end\":1778,\"text\":\"tumorigenesis\",\"entity\":\"DiseaseClass\",\"rationale\":\"'Tumorigenesis' denotes the biological process of tumor formation, not a disease. It is included as a disease-like term and excluded under the biological process rule.\",\"guideline_section\":\"What NOT to Annotate §5\",\"excluded\":\"yes\"}]}", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4357, "output_tokens": 15961, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 14260}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}